      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Proponents of \"race realism\" argue that IQ differences between groups are due to genetic variations among racial and ethnic groups, with certain groups possessing a genetic advantage that contributes to higher average IQs. They often cite the work of Hans Eysenck, who suggested that genetics play a significant role in determining intelligence, and argue that this is supported by the existence of heritable traits such as eye color and skin color. They also point to the fact that IQ scores tend to be higher in populations of European descent, which they claim is evidence of a genetic basis for intelligence. Additionally, they often reference the work of J.P. Rushton, who proposed that certain genetic variants are associated with higher IQs in certain populations.",
